Generalize '--' arg passthrough to all c2c client wrappers (c2c start/new <client> -- <client-opts>)

## Context

Introduced for codex under P1.M1.E1.T006: `c2c new codex -- <args>` forwards
everything after `--` verbatim to the codex frontend (e.g.
`c2c new codex -- --model gpt-5.3-codex-spark`), and we suggest a shell alias
ending in `--` (e.g. `cx='c2c new codex --'`) so `cx --model gpt-5.3-codex-spark`
Just Works. T006 owns this for codex ONLY.

## Objective

Make the `--` passthrough boundary consistent across ALL `c2c start`/`c2c new`
client wrappers (claude, codex, opencode, kimi, pi, gemini), not just codex.
Everything after `--` stops c2c's own option parsing and is forwarded verbatim
to the launched client's argv; nothing after `--` is ever interpreted as a c2c
flag. Only codex is currently *suggested* to be used this way, but the mechanism
should be uniform so any wrapped client behaves predictably.

## Acceptance criteria

- `c2c start <client> -- <opts...>` and `c2c new <client> -- <opts...>` forward
  `<opts...>` verbatim to the client for every supported client.
- `--` is the explicit, tested boundary; pre-`--` flags parse as c2c flags,
  post-`--` args never do (parser test per client, or one shared test).
- Suggested shell-alias convention (alias ending in `--`) documented generally
  in docs/commands.md, with codex as the primary worked example.
- No regression to existing per-client launch behavior or the existing
  `[client-options...]` handling.

## Notes

- Do this AFTER T006 lands (reuse/lift its codex passthrough implementation so
  there is one shared code path, not per-client copies).
- Origin: Max, 2026-07-11 — "it should work for any agent started with a c2c
  wrapper (though only codex is suggested to be used like that currently)."
